# Project Standards

## TypeScript Standards
- Use TypeScript for all code
- Avoid using `any` or `unknown` types
- Always provide explicit type definitions
- Use interfaces for object shapes
- Use type aliases for complex types
- Use generics when appropriate
- Use strict type checking

## Clean Code Standards
- All code must be in English
- Use self-explanatory variable and function names
- No comments explaining code functionality
- Break complex code into smaller, self-explanatory functions
- Follow single responsibility principle
- Use meaningful names that reveal intent
- Keep functions small and focused

## Atomic Design Structure
The project follows Atomic Design methodology with the following structure:

### Atoms
- Basic building blocks
- Located in `components/atoms/`
- Examples: buttons, inputs, typography, icons
- Must be highly reusable
- Should not contain business logic

### Molecules
- Combinations of atoms
- Located in `components/molecules/`
- Examples: form fields, search bars, navigation items
- Should be composed of multiple atoms
- Can contain simple logic

### Organisms
- Complex UI components
- Located in `components/organisms/`
- Examples: headers, footers, forms
- Can contain multiple molecules and atoms
- May contain business logic

### Templates
- Page layouts
- Located in `components/templates/`
- Define the structure of pages
- Placeholder for content
- No business logic

### Pages
- Final implementation
- Located in `app/` directory
- Use templates to render content
- Contain page-specific logic
- Handle data fetching

## File Organization
```
components/
  ├── atoms/
  │   ├── Button.tsx
  │   ├── Input.tsx
  │   └── Typography.tsx
  ├── molecules/
  │   ├── SearchBar.tsx
  │   └── FormField.tsx
  ├── organisms/
  │   ├── Header.tsx
  │   └── Footer.tsx
  └── templates/
      ├── MainLayout.tsx
      └── AuthLayout.tsx
app/
  ├── page.tsx
  └── (auth)/
      ├── login/
      │   └── page.tsx
      └── register/
          └── page.tsx
```

## Component Naming
- Use PascalCase for component names
- Use descriptive names that indicate the component's purpose
- Suffix with type: `Button.tsx`, `SearchBar.tsx`, `MainLayout.tsx`

## Type Definitions
- Place shared types in `types/` directory
- Use `.d.ts` files for global type declarations
- Use `.ts` files for type exports
- Group related types in the same file

## State Management
- Use React hooks for local state
- Use context for global state
- Keep state as close as possible to where it's used
- Avoid prop drilling

## Error Handling
- Use TypeScript for compile-time error prevention
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Use try-catch for runtime errors
- Provide meaningful error messages

## Testing
- Write tests for all components
- Use Jest and React Testing Library
- Test atoms in isolation
- Test molecules with their atom dependencies
- Test organisms with their molecule dependencies

## Testing Standards

### Unit Testing with Vitest
- Use Vitest for all unit tests
- Test files should be named `*.test.ts` or `*.test.tsx`
- Place test files next to the components they test
- Test atoms in isolation
- Test molecules with their atom dependencies
- Test organisms with their molecule dependencies
- Use `@testing-library/react` for component testing
- Follow AAA pattern (Arrange, Act, Assert)
- Mock external dependencies
- Test both success and error scenarios

### E2E Testing with Cypress
- Use Cypress for end-to-end testing
- Test files should be in `cypress/e2e/`
- Test critical user flows
- Test responsive design
- Test form submissions
- Test API integrations
- Test authentication flows
- Use custom commands for common operations
- Use fixtures for test data
- Follow best practices for Cypress testing

### Test Coverage
- Maintain minimum 80% code coverage
- Focus on critical business logic
- Test edge cases
- Test error handling
- Test accessibility features

### Test Organization
```
tests/
  ├── unit/
  │   ├── atoms/
  │   ├── molecules/
  │   └── organisms/
  └── e2e/
      ├── auth/
      ├── user/
      └── admin/
```

### Test Naming
- Use descriptive test names
- Follow pattern: `should [expected behavior] when [condition]`
- Group related tests using `describe` blocks
- Use `it` for individual test cases

### Test Data
- Use factories for test data
- Keep test data in `__fixtures__` directories
- Use meaningful test data
- Avoid hardcoding test values

### CI/CD Integration
- Run unit tests on every pull request
- Run E2E tests on main branch
- Generate coverage reports
- Block merges if tests fail
- Block merges if coverage drops below threshold
